  "report_markdown": "DRAFT\n\n**Complaint/History**\n- Itchy, sore, red skin for 4 days, primarily affecting chest, hands, and arms (including inside elbows).\n- Symptoms are significantly disruptive: interfering with sleep, work concentration, and weekend hiking.\n- Described as more widespread and itchy than previous eczema (which was limited to arms).\n- Skin is dry and cracked in some areas, but no bleeding or discharge reported.\n- No recent changes in shower gel, clothing, or known exposure to sick contacts.\n- Over-the-counter steroid cream and antihistamines tried without relief.\n- Symptoms started 4 days ago with no clear trigger identified.\n- No associated fever, respiratory, gastrointestinal, or urinary symptoms.\n\n**Relevant Background**\n- Past medical history: Eczema (previously managed with cream and shower product), past asthma (not current).\n- No regular medications.\n- No known allergies.\n- Lives with flatmates; works as a project manager in a pharmaceutical company.\n- Non-smoker; consumes a few beers per week.\n\n**Findings**\n- Not physically examined due to remote consultation; assessment based on patient description.\n- Described rash: red, itchy, sore, dry, cracked skin on chest, hands, arms, and inside elbows.\n- No facial involvement.\n- No systemic symptoms.\n\n**Clinician's Assessment**\n- Possible flare-up of eczema, though more widespread and severe than previous episodes.\n- Differential includes allergic contact dermatitis or other dermatitis, but eczema flare favored based on history.\n\n**Plan/Follow-up**\n- Prescribe stronger topical steroid (specific name not mentioned).\n- Prescribe emollient to use in bath/shower for moisturizing.\n- Prescribe stronger antihistamine (possibly fexofenadine, as phonetically interpreted).\n- Advised to use both steroid and emollient regularly for 7–10 days.\n- Advised to keep a trigger diary (e.g., soaps, fabrics).\n- Follow-up advised in one week if symptoms do not improve.",
